WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.130
Vsauce!

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:05.130
Kevin here, and this briefcase contains the
entire universe and everything that makes

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.220
humanity human.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.930
That’s it… this is it?

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:16.580
Paper, dollar, pencil, sugar cube, playing
card, Rubik’s cube?

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.539
Let me explain.

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:22.289
FOLD A PIECE OF PAPER.

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:30.579
Again… and again… a sheet of paper is
about 4-thousandths of an inch, and to get

00:00:30.579 --> 00:00:35.090
the number of layers after folding we make
2 to the power of folds.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:39.390
We start with 1, then every fold raises that
power one more.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.300
My folding is not going well!

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.750
The world record for paper folding is only
12 folds.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.430
If we could fold it 30 times, it’d be thick
enough to reach space.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:50.550
42 gets us to the moon.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:57.620
At 103 folds, the thickness of this paper
would be around 93 billion light years, so…

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:01.579
basically the diameter of our entire observable
universe.

00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:08.999
A piece of paper is the size of the universe.

00:01:08.999 --> 00:01:11.509
And you’re inside this sugar cube.

00:01:11.509 --> 00:01:14.440
In fact, we all are, or COULD be.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:18.299
Atoms are tiny, and their fundamental particles
are even smaller.

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:22.299
A proton is like… a femtometer, one quadrillionth
of a meter.

00:01:22.299 --> 00:01:26.080
The simple way to think of our body’s makeup
is that atoms are a collection of particles

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.540
with a fair amount of empty space between
them.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:33.079
Quantum physics shows us that’s not actually
true, and that a lot is happening in what

00:01:33.079 --> 00:01:37.270
appears to be 99.99999% empty space.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:39.340
That space matters.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:43.320
But if we compressed the atoms of the human
race into a tight ball and ignored what’s

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:49.279
in-between them, all nearly-8 billion of us
would fit inside this sugar cube.

00:01:49.279 --> 00:01:51.340
This is us.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:57.399
7.8 billion of us inside here, we can’t
all be unique, right?

00:01:57.399 --> 00:01:58.679
RIGHT!

00:01:58.679 --> 00:01:59.959
NO!

00:01:59.959 --> 00:02:01.249
WRONG!

00:02:01.249 --> 00:02:04.570
About 108 billion humans have been born throughout
history.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:09.780
If you’re 1 in a million like my mom told
me I am, that still means there have been

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:14.110
108,000 humans exactly like you.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:15.220
THAT’S NOT GOOD.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.030
How can anybody possibly stand out?

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:19.200
I KNOW.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.780
Just do something no human has ever done before…
like what?

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:24.840
Shuffle a deck of cards.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.970
Okay people have done that but not the same!

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:32.410
Michael mentioned this in his Math Magic video
it goes like this: You’ve got 52 cards in

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:33.410
a deck.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:42.140
The number of possible combinations is 52
factorial, so 52 x 51 x 50 x 49… yeah yeah

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.350
yeah, all the way down to one.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:51.040
That’s ehhhhhhh that number different orders
of a shuffled deck.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.400
Every time you shuffle and deal, you’ve
just created something that’s virtually

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:00.260
guaranteed to never have been done before
-- and seriously unlikely to ever be done

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:01.260
again.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:07.280
So ignore your 108,000 clones, opportunities
to be unique are actually everywhere.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:12.960
And there’s no limit to what you can do,
because you, my friend, you you you watchin’

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:18.950
this video, you’re a supercomputer.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:26.510
This Rubik’s Cube has 6 sides of a 3 by
3 grid, and each square is one of 6 colors.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:29.190
The question is: How many permutations exist?

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.370
That’s a good question, I think of it often.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:39.070
The answer is this: 43 quintillion, 252 quadrillion,
3 trillion, 274 billion, 489 million, 856

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:40.070
thousand.

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:41.070
That’s it.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:46.930
And every single possible state of the cube
can be solved in 20 moves or fewer.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:52.819
That’s called “God’s number,” and
it took Google 36 CPU-years of computing power

00:03:52.819 --> 00:03:53.840
to determine.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.790
Du Yusheng solved the classic 3x3x3 Rubik’s
Cube in…

00:03:57.790 --> 00:03:58.950
3.47 seconds.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:05.030
Maybe it takes you longer -- but you are mentally
capable of processing any one version of 43

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:10.310
quintillion problems and solving it in a surprisingly
low number of steps.

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:15.050
You solve problems in the world and if you’re
like me you go outside once a month to get

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:23.010
more peanut butter and you notice HEY there
are other people around.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:27.290
THIS helps connect us, but you’ve got to
think about it the right way.

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:33.180
This doesn’t actually have intrinsic value,
money isn’t actually worth anything -- it’s

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.250
paper.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:39.380
But it does represent a way for everyone to
exchange what they’ve got -- a thing, a

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:41.880
talent, whatever -- for what someone else
has.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.639
The Aztecs used cocoa beans and the ancient
Chinese used cowry shells.

00:04:45.639 --> 00:04:50.420
I’ve got a Playstation, I like making videos,
and I want a pygmy goat.

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:55.980
It’s a lot easier for me to use something
that allows me to transfer my old Playstation

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:01.820
and my video production skills, and then use
that to buy a pygmy goat, than it is to find

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:06.100
a pygmy goat farmer who needs a PS1 and a
paradox video.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:10.790
We have a system that converts what we’ve
got into what we want, and gives someone else

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:13.250
a way to get what they want with what they’ve
got.

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:18.730
And it’s not just about money, currencies
like love and trust and loyalty spend nicely,

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:19.730
too.

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:21.730
You can be you… with others.

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:29.250
That’s why we’re capable of making incredible
things.

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:30.790
Such as a #2 pencil.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:35.860
It’s like Leonard Read’s “I, Pencil”
-- it shows the sheer insanity of human cooperation

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:40.100
that results in making something as basic
as a pencil.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.280
Someone farmed the Incense cedar.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:44.730
Someone built the tractors for that farm.

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.200
Someone made the wheels for the tractor.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:52.060
Someone mined the graphite and clay for the
lead, others mined the metal for the ferrule.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:54.090
No one planned this.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:58.980
We just all… did our little piece, and others
combine those pieces with our needs and desires

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:04.199
to make a thing everyone uses, but no individual
person could ever make themselves.

00:06:04.199 --> 00:06:09.340
We can’t even comprehend what’s happening
in this sugar cube because it’s too complex.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:14.910
WHERE’S MY UNIVERSE PAPER?

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:19.169
We do all this because we know what 0 is.

00:06:19.169 --> 00:06:20.940
It’s not even just nothing.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:26.090
It’s the absence of value, value that exists
somewhere, just not where 0 is.

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:30.370
If infinity is so impossibly large that we
can’t even really understand it, 0 is an

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:35.350
impossibly-deep void we process mostly by
what it isn’t.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:41.190
We do understand the power of something that’s
a placeholder to make and identify greater

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:47.810
things, but that on its own is… sort of
a terrifying lack of meaning.

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:51.990
Zero makes the pencils happen, and it’s
the absence of pencils.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:58.340
It’s never getting what we want, it’s
never solving a Rubik’s Cube, it’s the

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:06.370
inability to be unique, it’s inaction, it’s
an empty sugar cube -- it’s being in this

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:12.960
unfathomably large paper universe and fighting
against the prospect of being and meaning

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:14.760
nothing.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:20.600
You know what it is, but you’re everything
because you’re not zero.

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:24.760
The world is big, the universe is complex.

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:30.199
And you can visualize, conceptualize, and
appreciate some of the most complicated elements

00:07:30.199 --> 00:07:36.900
of life by studying, and researching, and
experimenting -- but also by messin’ around

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:41.360
with some of the junk lying around your bedroom.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:43.690
OR STORED IN YOUR EXTREMELY COOL BRIEFCASE.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:46.610
And as always, thanks for watching.

